UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 1: Listening to music and responding', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a reason from the music, such as ''The slow tempo and soft dynamics make it feel calm.'' Different people may feel different things. It is respectful to listen to the whole piece quietly before responding.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA piece is played slowly on a low cello and gets softer at the end. Describe two elements you hear and say what mood they might create.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is linked to a feeling, which is a stronger response than just ''it is sad''.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nListen to three short pieces with an adult, such as a lullaby, a march and a piece from your own choice. For each write the mood, two elements you heard and one reason. Draw a picture of the mood for your favourite.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a reason from the music, such as ''The slow tempo and soft dynamics make it feel calm.'' Different people may feel different things. It is respectful to listen to the whole piece quietly before responding.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Listen to three short pieces with an adult, such as a lullaby, a march and a piece from your own choice. For each write the mood, two elements you heard and one reason. Draw a picture of the mood for your favourite. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is linked to a feeling, which is a… Reasoning: I used listening by attending to an element of music rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. Correction: Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is… Why: the correction restores accurate use of listening.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating listening on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with listening, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is… This matches the lesson skill for listening.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is…","Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear."],"question":"A piece is played slowly on a low cello and gets softer at the end. Describe two elements you hear and say what mood they might create. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of an element of music with clear evidence.","Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear.","Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel.…","Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named an element of music, not by copying the model.","options":["Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the an element of music used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising listening. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is…","Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about listening.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about an element of music?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and the drums are…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear.","Listen to three short pieces with an adult, such as a lullaby, a march and a piece from your own choice. For each write the mood, two elements you…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating listening on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is linked to a feeling, which is a… Reasoning: I used listening by attending to an element of music rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with listening, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. Correction: Strong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is… Why: the correction restores accurate use of listening.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w10-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise listening.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use listening with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify an element of music and explain why it matters.","I can practise a mood independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a music response needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a reason from the music, such as ''The slow tempo and soft dynamics make it feel calm.'' Different people may feel different things. It is respectful to listen to the whole piece quietly before responding.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and the drums are forte, so it feels exciting and scary. The high pitched flute sounds like lightning.'' He names tempo, dynamics, instruments and pitch to explain the mood.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA piece is played slowly on a low cello and gets softer at the end. Describe two elements you hear and say what mood they might create.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is linked to a feeling, which is a stronger response than just ''it is sad''.","explain":"Listening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a reason from the music, such as ''The slow tempo and soft dynamics make it feel calm.'' Different people may feel different things. It is respectful to listen to the whole piece quietly before responding.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Listening in two steps.","caption":"Mood, then elements.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Listen once</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">feel the mood</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Listen twice</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">name the elements</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and t Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and t Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and the drums are forte, so it feels exciting and scary. The high pitched flute sounds like lightning.'' He names tempo, dynamics, instruments and pitch to explain the mood. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Elements make a mood.","caption":"Link sound to feeling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Element</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow tempo, soft sound</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Mood</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">calm, sleepy</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and the drums are forte, so it feels exciting and scary. The high pitched flute sounds like lightning.'' He names tempo, dynamics, instruments and pitch to explain the mood.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''The slow tempo and the low pitch of the cello feel sad and heavy, and the diminuendo at the end feels like it is fading away.'' Each element is linked to a feeling, which is a stronger response than just ''it is sad''.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking there is a right or wrong feeling about music. Feelings can differ, but a good response gives a reason that comes from what you hear. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nListening to music carefully means noticing the elements, such as beat, rhythm, pitch, dynamics, tempo and instruments, and how they make the piece feel. Listen once for the mood, and again for the elements. Describe what you hear and then say how it makes you feel, giving a reason from the music, such as ''The slow tempo and soft dynamics make it feel calm.'' Different people may feel different things. It is respectful to listen to the whole piece quietly before responding. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 1: Listening to music and responding","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Beat, rhythm and pitch","week":10,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ben listens to a short piece about a storm. First time: ''It feels scary.'' Second time: ''The tempo is fast and the drums are forte, so it feels exciting and scary. The high pitched flute sounds like lightning.'' He names tempo, dynamics, instruments and pitch to explain the mood.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Listening in two steps.","caption":"Mood, then elements.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Listen once</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">feel the mood</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Listen twice</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">name the elements</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Elements make a mood.","caption":"Link sound to feeling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Element</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow tempo, soft sound</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Mood</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">calm, sleepy</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w10-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
